post-publication test-harness correction:
  The initial direct five-suite execution against the accepted repository
  state produced 881 PASS and 8 failures. The failures were limited to the
  pre-publication test lifecycle: one test directly asserted that the real
  canonical report path was absent, one synthetic predecessor positive proof
  observed the accepted report, and the six parameterized predecessor attack
  cases could not establish their synthetic positive baseline for the same
  reason. Production behavior and accepted evidence were not defective.

  The committed A1 pytest harness now uses a function-scoped autouse fixture
  to bind the runner module's canonical safe-report path to a fresh absent
  tmp_path file for each test. Tests that exercise canonical-path attacks may
  still replace that isolated path with their own controlled temporary path.
  The fixture does not alter production constants, runtime behavior, the
  accepted report, or the existing zero-network/provider sentinel.

  After the correction, the exact five-suite command was executed directly,
  without an audit-time plugin, in-memory command wrapper, path environment
  override, or temporary conftest. It produced 889 PASS, zero failures, zero
  skips, and zero xfails. The accepted report remained present, mode 0400,
  byte-identical, and descriptor-verifiable before and after both runs.

